    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. I'm looking at a specific case where the interruption wasn't a disaster like a flood, but more of an unpredictable disruption to the supply chain and local accessibility that made the retail space effectively unusable for the tenant, even though the building itself was fine. Does the concept of 'unforeseeability' in these clauses usually cover these kinds of secondary, indirect disruptions, or is it strictly limited to the event itself?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Singapore, the scope of 'unforeseeability' as it pertains to force majeure clauses in commercial leases typically covers reasonably foreseeable repercussions related to the primary event. While traditional disruption such as floods or earthquakes are explicitly included, the clause must precisely define what constitutes as a force majeure. To determine if supply chain interruptions and local accessibility issues, like those experienced by your retail space, fall under force majeure, it is essential to analyze the specific language and intent of the clause. It often depends on legal interpretation whether secondary and indirect disruptions fall within the contractual protection. Courts in Singapore tend to closely examine the literal terms and the underlying intent of the parties, along with any relevant case law, when adjudicating force majeure claims. Therefore, consulting with a legal professional will be indispensable to ascertain how such specific circumstances are interpreted in relation to your lease agreement. Additionally, reviewing any local regulations or precedents concerning economic disruptions might provide further insight into how these secondary effects might be covered under force majeure.",
      "source": "target",
