      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. So it really comes down to the phrasing. I'm wondering about the \"unpredictability\" aspect though. If a disruption is caused by a series of chaotic, seemingly random logistical failures that look more like pure bad luck or even \"mischief\" in the system rather than a single massive event, how would a court view that? Would they see that lack of a clear, single cause as making the event more or less likely to qualify as force majeure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The concept of unpredictability in the context of a force majeure clause and an event arising from a series of chaotic, seemingly random logistical failures could present a complex challenge for a court in Singapore. The crux of force majeure is that the event must be unforeseeable and beyond the control of the affected party. When such events lack a clear, single cause but arise from a confluence of circumstances, it becomes a question of whether these interrelated events collectively are an unforeseeable obstacle to the tenant's contractual obligations.\n\nIf the chain of events was entirely unpredictable and beyond the reasonable anticipation of the parties when forming the lease agreement, a court in Singapore could potentially see it as an unforeseeable disruption, thus classifying it under force majeure. The absence of a single cause does not inherently preclude an event from qualifying; rather, it is the unforeseeable nature of the disruption that is key.\n\nHowever, the suddenness, randomness, and the degree to which external factors made the disaster beyond the tenant's control would also be crucial in such a determination. Courts would consider whether the cumulative effect of these failures on operations was as material and significant as other more direct or clear-cut force majeure events.\n\nUltimately, it would be contingent on how the",
